[2026-10-31T23:37:28.267Z] PALIMPSEST_DELEGATION {"delivered":true,"text":"Delegated research completed.\nBasis: 8034ee861740\nExploratory conclusion: The frozen basis contains no scan-cycle-time or throughput specification for the Filmtek scanner (sources/vendors.md gives only its 15–20°C/40–50% RH operating envelope, £1,200 day rate, two-week minimum booking and £1,800-each-way transport; sources/condition.md and sources/sites.md give only the handling rule), so cycle time must be calibrated externally: a professional 35mm nitrate scan realistically runs ~20–40 min per reel end-to-end (a 1,000 ft reel is ~11 min of film at 24 fps, so 2K/4K scanning plus inspection, loading, threading and unloading dominates). The conservation guidance actually present is: keep reels in the 4°C/35% RH cold store whenever not being scanned, use the adjacent 16°C/45% RH inspection room and its pass-through hatch (which removes the need for acclimatisation), and scan at 15–20°C and below 50% RH, since Filmtek cannot operate inside the cold store (sources/sites.md, sources/condition.md, sources/vendors.md). On that calibration, the 240-reel/10-working-day plan is not feasible under the policy that no more than 40 reels leave the cold store for more than 30 minutes including scanning: 40 reels per 30-minute window implies ≤45 seconds per reel, whereas one reel alone consumes most or all of a 30-minute window, so the rule caps throughput at roughly 1–2 reels per window (≈16–32 reels per 8-hour day) and 240 reels would need on the order of 15+ working days even before the orphan-works rights assessment (about three weeks of staff time per reel set, sources/rights.md) and the two-week scanner booking are considered.\nStanding: cell-local exploratory finding; not Work Evidence or Project Verification."}
